<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.jkrumm.lock-at-boot</string>
  <!-- Locks the screen immediately after the unattended auto-login.
       FileVault is OFF and automatic login is ON so the mini reboots itself
       after a power cut (see docs/remote-dev.md). That is what makes the login
       keychain reachable — Claude Code's Max OAuth credential lives there and
       is only readable from a GUI-session process — but it also means a fresh
       boot lands on an UNLOCKED desktop. This agent closes that window.

       Screen lock does NOT lock the keychain. The login keychain re-locks on
       exactly three events: the "Lock when sleeping" setting, the "Lock after
       N minutes" inactivity timer, and logout. Screen lock, screensaver and
       fast user switching are none of them. So the session keeps running fully
       — herdr, the agents, every LaunchAgent — behind a password prompt.
       Verified against security(1) and Apple's keychain docs, 2026-08-01. -->

  <!-- WHY pmset AND NOT osascript. The obvious "lock now" is a synthesized
       Ctrl-Cmd-Q via System Events, but that needs Accessibility (TCC), which
       cannot be granted to a launchd job on a headless machine — there is
       nobody to click Allow. `pmset displaysleepnow` needs no TCC at all.
       It only locks because `sysadminctl -screenLock immediate` removes the
       grace period; without that this agent sleeps the display and leaves the
       session unlocked for the configured delay. make lock-at-boot-setup
       refuses to install unless that setting is already in place.

       CGSession -suspend is NOT an option: the User.menu bundle it lives in was
       removed from macOS and is absent on 26.5.2 (checked on this disk, not
       inferred — most write-ups online still recommend it). -->

  <!-- WHY THE DELAY. RunAtLoad fires as the session is still coming up, and a
       displaysleepnow issued mid-handoff from loginwindow can no-op silently.
       Five seconds is negligible next to the screensaver idle timer that backs
       this up, and a no-op here would be invisible without the assertion below. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>sleep 5; /usr/bin/pmset displaysleepnow; sleep 3; if /usr/sbin/ioreg -n Root -d1 -a | /usr/bin/grep -q CGSSessionScreenIsLocked; then echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S') locked"; else echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S') NOT LOCKED - check: sysadminctl -screenLock status"; fi</string>
  </array>
  <!-- One-shot, so no KeepAlive: this must fire once per session start, not be
       resurrected. Resuming a suspended session over Screen Sharing does not
       start a new session, so unlocking from the MacBook does not re-trigger
       it — you are not locked back out mid-work. -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <!-- ~/Library/Logs, never /tmp: macOS's periodic cleanup DELETES /tmp files,
       and launchd opens these paths once at spawn, so a swept log leaves the
       agent writing into an unlinked inode. Rotated by scripts/log-rotate.sh,
       which carries this file in its declared list. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/lock-at-boot.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/lock-at-boot.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
